
  One thing I hit immediately while reading the code, and it's the question I need answered before I can write the
  change:

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is called from the form submit handler, which only has the two form field values.
  There is no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in the codebase for that caller to pass. So "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter" has more than one
  coherent meaning, and they produce different interfaces:

  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is really the API's response.[0m In most login flows the user's ID is what the server [3mreturns[0m after
    authenticating — the client can't know it beforehand. This would mean changing the return value ([38;5;153m{ success, user, [39m
    [38;5;153muserId }[39m) and logging it after the call, not adding a parameter. Buys you a real identity to track; costs nothing at
    the call site; rules out nothing. Cheap to change later.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is a client-generated correlation ID.[0m The handler generates something ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) per attempt and
    passes it in, so a login attempt can be traced even when it fails. Buys you trace coverage of failed attempts; costs
    a new ID-generation decision; this is the one that's awkward to change later, since the ID's format becomes part of
    the log contract.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m comes from somewhere I can't see[0m — an existing session, config, or a caller outside this repo. Then I need to
    know where, and the signature change is genuinely a caller-visible break I'd want to confirm first.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first one.[0m It's what "track who logged in" usually means, [38;5;153musername[39m already identifies the
  attempt for the logging you have today, and it doesn't put an invented ID into your call signature. But I'd rather
  hear it from you than guess — if you have a real [38;5;153muserId[39m source in mind, that changes the design.
